course: french
lesson: 9
title: Colours
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: French colours sit behind the thing they describe, so a red ball is un ballon rouge, "a ball red", like a label stuck on after the name.
explain: Colours also change their spelling to match the thing: a green jumper is un pull vert, but a green skirt is une jupe verte, because jupe is a feminine word.
explain: Picture the French flag on a pole: bleu, blanc, rouge, in that order from the pole outwards, and French people often say the three together like one word.
explain: Some colours never change: those already ending in e, like rouge and jaune, keep the same form, and marron and orange, named after a chestnut and a fruit, never change at all.
explain: Hair has its own words: a girl with dark hair is brune, and ginger hair is roux or rousse, never rouge.
fact: The French flag is bleu, blanc, rouge ("BLUH, BLAHN, ROOZH"): blue, white, red, in that order from the flagpole.
fact: Colour words come after the noun in French: un ballon rouge (a red ball), un chat noir (a black cat), une voiture bleue (a blue car).
fact: With a feminine noun most colours add -e: vert becomes verte, and then the t is said: un pull vert ("VAIR"), une jupe verte ("VAIRT").
fact: bleu means blue and is said "BLUH" with rounded lips, not "bloo"; bleue sounds the same.
fact: A few colours change more: blanc ("BLAHN") becomes blanche ("BLAHNSH"), and violet becomes violette.
fact: marron (brown) and orange never change, not even in the plural: des chaussures marron (brown shoes).
fact: jaune ("ZHOHN") means yellow; it already ends in e, so it is the same with un and une: un ballon jaune, une fleur jaune.
fact: For hair: brun / brune means dark-haired, blond / blonde means blond, roux / rousse means ginger; cheveux rouges would mean hair dyed bright red.
fact: rose ("ROZ") means pink and is also the flower une rose; as a colour it takes an s in the plural: des tee-shirts roses.
fact: gris ("GREE") means grey, not green; the s is silent; with a feminine noun it is grise ("GREEZ"); green is vert.
fact: rouge means red and already ends in e: une pomme rouge, un camion rouge.
fact: noir is the masculine and noire the feminine; both sound "NWAR": un chien noir, une souris noire.
fact: Slang here: "posh" (UK) means fancy; "like" at the end (Yorkshire) adds a shrug; "whit" (Scotland) means what; "gonna" means going to; "loses it" means laughs a lot; "eh" (Canada) asks you to agree; "sweet as" (New Zealand) means great; "wee" (Scotland) means small.
exercise: What are the colours of the French flag, in French? | bleu, blanc, rouge | blue, blanche, rouge | bleu, vert, rouge | rouge, blanc, noir
exercise: Oscar says "un rouge ballon". What is right? | un ballon rouge | une ballon rouge | un ballon rouges | une rouge ballon
exercise: Freya writes "une jupe vert". What should it be? | une jupe verte | un jupe vert | une verte jupe | une jupe vertes
exercise: How do you say vert in un pull vert? | "VAIR", with the t silent | "VAIRT", with a clear t | "VURT", like in English | "VAIR-tuh", with an extra sound
exercise: How should Riley say bleu? | "BLUH", with rounded lips | "BLOO", like English blue | "BLAY", like a blade | "BLEW", rhyming with "few"
exercise: Tilly draws "une maison blanc". What is right? | une maison blanche | une maison blance | une maison blancs | un maison blanc
exercise: Which pair is right? | violet / violette | violet / violete | violette / violetteu | violets / violet
exercise: Ruby writes "des chaussures marrons". What's right? | des chaussures marron | des chaussures marronnes | des chaussure marrons | des chaussures marronne
exercise: How do you say jaune? | "ZHOHN", soft zh and a long oh | "JOHN", like the boy's name | "YAWN", like a sleepy face | "ZHAWN-ay", with an extra sound
exercise: Grace has dark hair. What does her penpal write? | Tu es brune ! | Tu es brun ! | Tu es rouge ! | Tu es marron !
exercise: How do you say "a ginger boy" in French? | un garçon roux | un garçon rouge | un garçon orange | un garçon rousse
exercise: What colour is rose? | pink | red | purple | orange
exercise: How do you write "pink T-shirts"? | des tee-shirts roses | des tee-shirts rose | des tee-shirt roses | un tee-shirts roses
exercise: Daisy hears "gris". What colour is it? | grey | green | black | blue
exercise: How do you write "a red apple"? | une pomme rouge | une pomme rougee | une rouge pomme | un pomme rouge
exercise: Hannah says "un chien noire". What is right? | un chien noir | une chien noire | un chien noirs | un chien noire
exercise: Which colours never change at all, even in the plural? | marron and orange | rouge and jaune | vert and bleu | noir and blanc
test: Amelia jokes the flag is "blue, white, red, but posh." ("Posh" means fancy.) How would she say it, and in what order? | bleu, blanc, rouge, from the flagpole outwards | rouge, blanc, bleu, from the flagpole outwards | bleu, rouge, blanc, from the top down | blanc, bleu, rouge, from the bottom up
test: Oscar says, "That's backwards, like." What does French do with colours? | It puts the colour after the noun | It puts the colour before the noun | It leaves the colour out | It puts the colour at the end of the sentence
test: Freya says, "Whit? It's still green." ("Whit" means what.) Why did "une jupe vert" get a red mark? | jupe is feminine, so the colour needs an e: verte | vert is only for boys' clothes in France | Skirts can never be green in French | The colour should come before the noun
test: Riley says, "I'm gonna say 'bloo'." What's the problem? | bleu has a rounded "uh" sound, not "oo" | bleu ends in a "ks" sound | bleu is said "blay" | Nothing, it's exactly the English sound
test: Tilly asks, "Wait, it's a whole different word?" about blanche. What's true? | blanc changes more than just adding e: blanche | She is wrong: it is blance | blanche means pink | blanche is only used for snow
test: Ruby says, "Sure that's just mean," when her teacher crosses out the s on marrons. What's the reason? | marron never changes, not even in the plural | Shoes are always singular in French | marron should be brune for shoes | The s was in the wrong place
test: A French boy hears Lucas say "John" and hands him his friend Jean. Everyone loses it. ("Loses it" means laughs a lot.) What did Lucas want? | The yellow pencil, jaune | The pencil of a boy called John | A brown pencil, marron | A white pencil, blanc
test: Grace asks, "Eh, did I get my own hair colour wrong?" when her penpal writes "Tu es brune !" ("Eh" asks you to agree.) What happened? | Her penpal used the girl's form for dark hair | Her penpal thinks she has grey hair | Her penpal is saying she is sunburnt | Her penpal made a spelling mistake
test: Leo says, "Sweet as, one word for two things." ("Sweet as" means great.) Which word does he mean? | rose: pink, and the flower | noir: black, and the night | bleu: blue, and the sky | vert: green, and grass
test: Daisy shouts, "It's not green, it's grey, man!" What does gris mean? | Grey, so the French were right | Green, so Daisy was right | Grey or green, both | Black, so they were both wrong
test: ★★★ Which phrase has NO mistake? | une souris noire et un chat blanc | une souris noir et un chat blanc | un souris noire et un chat blanche | une souris noire et un chat blanche
test: ★★★ Blue paint and yellow paint mixed make a new colour. How do you write "a leaf" of that colour? (A leaf is une feuille.) | une feuille verte | une feuille vert | un feuille verte | une verte feuille
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the position of colour adjectives in French? | They are customarily placed after the noun they qualify | They are customarily placed before the noun they qualify | They are placed at the close of the sentence | They are omitted where the colour is evident
exam: Identify the correct rendering of "a black mouse". | une souris noire | une souris noir | un souris noire | une noire souris
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the adjectives marron and orange. | They remain invariable in gender and in number | They add an e for the feminine and an s for the plural | They change wholly into other words for feminine nouns | They are employed solely before the noun
exam: It is given that a girl possesses ginger hair. Determine the adjective appropriate to her. | rousse | rouge | roux | orange
